"A saga choreography diagram shows participants advancing a distributed business transaction by reacting to events without a central coordinator.\n\nUse when: Review event-driven business consistency across services. Expose event chains and compensation responsibilities.\n\nAvoid when: A single coordinator is responsible for deciding every step.\n\nVisual grammar: Participants own local transactions and their stores. Directed event paths identify facts emitted and consumed. Compensating event paths and correlation identifiers connect failure recovery to the same saga.\n\nBuild: Define the business outcome and participating services. Write each participant’s input event and resulting local effect. Add emitted events, failure outcomes, and compensation reactions. Lay out the event chain with a separate recovery corridor and readable broker boundaries.\n\nCheck: No hidden central controller is implied by broker placement. Event names describe facts rather than disguised unlabeled commands. Correlation and duplicate handling survive the complete chain. Recovery paths avoid event labels and participant text.\n\nPitfalls: Assuming choreography means there is no workflow ownership. Omitting cyclic event reactions or compensation loops from the review.\n\nExample: OrderCreated triggers a stock hold; StockHeld triggers payment; PaymentFailed causes Inventory to release the hold.\n\nAlternatives: Publish-subscribe diagram, Saga orchestration diagram."
